	(gr_text "Note: T7 - T12 (large pad test points) and LED \nsignals use thin traces because they don’t need \nmuch current. J20 uses thick traces for power\nlines so that it *could* be used for higher current\nexternal applications"
		(at -42.4688 73.025 0)
		(layer "Dwgs.User")
		(uuid "00000000-0000-0000-0000-0000640d24b6")
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.5 1.5)
				(thickness 0.3)
			)
		)
	)
	(gr_text "Note: J30 uses thick traces for power\nlines so that it *could* be used for higher current\nexternal applications"
		(at 113.411 -114.3 0)
		(layer "Dwgs.User")
		(uuid "00000000-0000-0000-0000-0000640fda34")
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.5 1.5)
				(thickness 0.3)
			)
		)
	)
	(gr_text "Note: manually added some extra thicc traces \non some of the GND_motor pads, where \nappropriate for handling a lot of current"
		(at 178.4096 -15.5956 0)
		(layer "Dwgs.User")
		(uuid "00000000-0000-0000-0000-0000640d6a49")
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.5 1.5)
				(thickness 0.3)
			)
		)
	)
	(gr_text "Note: it’s fine that the 5V plane is a little thin up \nhere in the motor connector pandhandle part \nof the board. Only the corner servos draw their \nmotor current from 5V, whereas the drive motors \ndraw motor current from the roboclaw RC*M*A/B \nlines. The thinest bit, about 2.5mm, should be more \nthan enough copper to handle the current."
		(at 182.4482 -54.991 0)
		(layer "Dwgs.User")
		(uuid "005ec5b4-3cba-4e5b-b768-9a15b039eafd")
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.5 1.5)
				(thickness 0.3)
			)
		)
	)
